{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CetLn76RxgN33miVqGtS5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my exploration, I have a good understanding of the codebase structure and requirements. Let me now proceed to the design phase.\n\n```\n<<PHASE:DESIGN>>\n```\n\n**Proposed change, file by file:**\n\n1. **Order.kt**: Add an `OrderStatus` enum with `ACTIVE` and `CANCELLED` values. Add a `status: OrderStatus = OrderStatus.ACTIVE` field to the Order data class to maintain backward compatibility when creating orders.\n\n2. **OrderController.kt**: Add a new endpoint `@PostMapping(\"/{orderId}/cancel\")` that:\n   - Finds the order by ID (404 if not found)\n   - If already CANCELLED, return it (idempotent)\n   - Check all shipments for this order; if any are CONFIRMED, throw ConflictException with 409\n   - Cancel all CREATED shipments (set status to CANCELLED)\n   - Set order status to CANCELLED and save\n   - Return the cancelled order with 200 OK\n\n3. **OrderRepository.kt**: No changes needed - the `save()` method already updates the order.\n\n4. **ShipmentRepository.kt**: Add a `findByOrderId(orderId: String)` method to retrieve all shipments for an order by querying the store.\n\n5. **ShipmentController.kt**: When creating a shipment, check if the order exists and is CANCELLED. If CANCELLED, throw ConflictException with 409. If order doesn't exist, silently proceed (to maintain \"Creating a shipment for an order that does not exist keeps its current behaviour\").\n\n6. **ApiError.kt**: Add new error codes:\n   - `ORDER_CANCELLED` - for when trying to create a shipment for a cancelled order\n   - `ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT` - for when trying to cancel an order with a confirmed shipment\n\n7. **OrderControllerTest.kt**: Add tests for:\n   - Cancelling an active order succeeds and returns the order with CANCELLED status\n   - Cancelling an active order cancels all its CREATED shipments\n   - Cancelling an already cancelled order succeeds with no changes (idempotency)\n   - Cancelling an order with a CONFIRMED shipment fails with 409 and changes nothing\n   - Cancelling a non-existent order fails with 404\n\n8. **ShipmentControllerTest.kt**: Add test for:\n   - Creating a shipment for a cancelled order fails with 409\n\n**Alternative considered and rejected:**\n\n- **Alternative**: Add a separate `OrderService` class to handle the cancel business logic. **Rejected** because the existing code uses thin controllers that directly interact with repositories, not service classes. Adding a service layer would be unrelated architectural change and against the requirement to \"preserve the existing Kotlin + Spring Boot architecture\".\n\n- **Alternative**: Add an enum extension function `cancel()` on ShipmentStatus. **Rejected** because the status is immutable (in the Shipment data class), so changing it requires creating a new Shipment object and saving it.\n\n**Data and error flow:**\n\n1. **Cancel ACTIVE order with CREATED shipments**:\n   - Read: Find order by ID, find all shipments by orderId, check their statuses\n   - Check: If any shipment is CONFIRMED, throw ConflictException(ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT, 409) and return early - nothing written\n   - Write: Update all CREATED shipments to CANCELLED, update order to CANCELLED\n   - Return: 200 OK with the cancelled order\n\n2. **Cancel already CANCELLED order**:\n   - Read: Find order by ID\n   - Check: If status is already CANCELLED, return immediately\n   - Write: None\n   - Return: 200 OK with the same order\n\n3. **Cancel non-existent order**:\n   - Read: Try to find order by ID\n   - Check: Not found\n   - Write: None\n   - Return: 404 with ORDER_NOT_FOUND\n\n4. **Create shipment for CANCELLED order**:\n   - Read: Find order by orderId (through a query or check in controller)\n   - Check: If order exists and is CANCELLED, throw ConflictException(ORDER_CANCELLED, 409)\n   - Write: If check passes, create shipment as before\n   - Return: 201 with the new shipment, or 409 if order is CANCELLED\n\n**Test strategy:**\n\n- **Test 1** (OrderControllerTest): Cancel an active order → expects 200, order status is CANCELLED, shipments are cancelled\n- **Test 2** (OrderControllerTest): Cancel an already-cancelled order → expects 200, nothing changes\n- **Test 3** (OrderControllerTest): Cancel order with CONFIRMED shipment → expects 409, order stays ACTIVE, shipment statuses unchanged\n- **Test 4** (OrderControllerTest): Cancel non-existent order → expects 404 with ORDER_NOT_FOUND\n- **Test 5** (ShipmentControllerTest): Create shipment for cancelled order → expects 409 with ORDER_CANCELLED\n\n---\n\n```\n<<PHASE:IMPLEMENTATION>>\n```\n\nNow I'll implement the design. Let me start by modifying the Order model to add the status field:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1817,"cache_read_input_tokens":22581,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1817},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f8c01f9-32ca-48ee-9404-e2af86c81a87","uuid":"506641ce-4204-4bdc-8414-73026aa6ea70","timestamp":"2026-09-09T18:30:57.003Z","request_id":"req_011CetLn6SEb7Gr8Vw79YXo7"}
